---
// The home page. The first screen follows the tracker pattern's section 1.3 (ruled by John
// 2026-10-16) in two columns, as the Ledger skin's proposal laid it out and John ruled on
// 2026-10-17: left, the eyebrow, the computed year in the largest type with its claim and the three
// statistics under it (the chance before 2050, the nine-in-ten span, the share not this century),
// the question as the page title, one paragraph, the last-checked strip, two buttons; right, the
// chart of the spread of escape years (Spread.astro), the trunk's picture, standing between double
// rules as "Sheet I". The first-screen markup is the design session's (merged from main on
// 2026-10-17); everything below the first screen is the site session's: what a treatment is, the
// eight branches with live counts and the year each is handled in the middle run, the six kinds of
// record with live counts, who is asking. Written for a reader who has never heard of any of this
// (design standard, section 1a); UK spelling throughout. Every number comes from records.json
// (scripts/export.py), which reads the computed headline from data/headline.json; nothing here is
// typed in. The strip and the branch cards are copied from The Belt Equation and The Upgrade Tree
// (tracker pattern, section 7).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Spread from '../components/Spread.astro';
import { records, longDate, plural, percentWords, spreadWords, yearWords, REPO } from '../lib/records';

const c = records.counts;
const h = records.headline;
const reading = records.reading;
const branches = records.branches;
const fixed = records.fixed_dates;
const loneMover = reading && reading.movers.length === 1 ? reading.movers[0] : null;
---

<Base>
  <div class="home-first">
  <section class="home-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">A public tracker of the race between repair and ageing</p>
    {h.available ? (
      <p class="big-number">{h.median_year}</p>
    ) : (
      <p class="big-number pending">Not computed yet</p>
    )}
    <p class="big-claim-line">
      {h.available
        ? `The middle estimate of the year medicine starts adding more than a year of healthy life for every year that passes.`
        : `The year medicine starts adding more than a year of healthy life for every year that passes, worked out from the record and never chosen by hand, will stand here.`}
    </p>
    {h.available && (
      <div class="stat-row" aria-label="The three numbers beside the year">
        <div><span class="stat">{(h.before_2050 * 100).toFixed(1)}%</span><span class="stat-label">chance it arrives before {fixed.before}</span></div>
        <div><span class="stat">{h.percentiles.p5} to {yearWords(h.percentiles.p95)}</span><span class="stat-label">in nine runs of ten</span></div>
        <div><span class="stat">{Math.round(h.after_2100 * 100)}%</span><span class="stat-label">not this century</span></div>
      </div>
    )}
    <h1 class="question">When do we start outrunning ageing?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every year you live, medicine gets a little better. Right now it does not get better fast enough: for a person in middle age, the years they can expect to have left fall by about a year every year, less the small gains new treatments add. This site asks when, if ever, that stops being true: when repair overtakes ageing, so that someone who keeps up with the best available care finds their remaining years holding steady or rising instead of falling.
    </p>
    <p class="scan-strip">
      <span class="strip-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span>
        {reading
          ? <>Last checked {longDate(reading.read_on)}: {plural(reading.leaves_read, 'treatment', 'treatments')}, {plural(reading.claims_read, 'claim', 'claims')} and {plural(reading.experiments_read, 'self-experiment', 'self-experiments')} read; {reading.moved.length === 0 ? 'none moved' : reading.moved.length === 1 ? 'one moved a rung' : `${reading.moved.length} moved a rung`}{loneMover ? <>: <a href={loneMover.path}>{loneMover.name}</a></> : ''}{reading.waiting > 0 ? `; ${reading.waiting} left for the author to decide` : ''}. <a href={reading.path}>See the week's reading.</a></>
          : <>No reading of the week's news against the treatments has run yet. The first one follows the site's launch, and this line will say when it ran and what came of it. <a href="/reading/">About the weekly reading.</a></>}
      </span>
    </p>
    <p class="ctas">
      <a class="btn" href="/branches/">What has to happen</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="#who">Who is asking</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="first-side figure-card">
    <p class="plate-label">Sheet I · The spread of escape years</p>
    <Spread />
    <ul class="legend">
      <li><i class="bars"></i> share of runs landing in each five-year span</li>
      <li><i class="before"></i> before {fixed.before}, the date the loudest promises name</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="figure-note">The tree is played out two hundred thousand times, each time drawing a year for every treatment and tool. Each run's answer is the year the last required branch is handled, plus the years it takes a treatment to reach ordinary people. Nothing on the chart is typed in.</p>
  </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section explain narrow">
    <p>
      A <em>treatment</em>, here, is one specific thing that would have to arrive for the year to move: a drug, a procedure, a gene therapy, or a measurement a regulator accepts as proof that something slowed ageing. Each one is written down in the same parts, so that a year can be worked out from them and anyone who disagrees can point at the exact part: a plain test of what would count, written so that two readers would agree; who or what settles that test; where it stands today on a five-rung ladder from idea only to in wide use, with the date and the public record for every rung reached; and the project's estimate of the year it reaches the clinic, as a range with the reasoning beside it. The eight <em>branches</em> are the eight things that would have to be dealt with: seven kinds of damage that build up in a body over a lifetime, and the tools that would show whether any repair worked. Beside the year the site keeps a dated record of who promised what and when, held against what has actually been shown.
    </p>
    {h.available ? (
      <p>
        None of the numbers on the first screen is typed in. The tree of {plural(c.leaves, 'treatment and tool', 'treatments and tools')} is played out {h.method.runs.toLocaleString('en-GB')} times, drawing a year for each from its range; the middle of all those runs is the year at the top, and the numbers beside it and every mark on the chart are read off the same spread of results, with {spreadWords()}. {h.counts.estimates_first_pass_drawn === 0 ? `Every range the year draws from has been through an outside review, in which an AI model made by another company argued each one up or down and the author ruled on every disagreement${h.counts.estimates_first_pass > 0 ? `; ${plural(h.counts.estimates_first_pass, 'estimate', 'estimates')} for a rung the year does not draw ${h.counts.estimates_first_pass === 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} still a first pass` : ''}. The year will still move as the evidence does, and every move is dated with its reason.` : `${plural(h.counts.estimates_first_pass_drawn, 'estimate the year draws from is', 'estimates the year draws from are')} still a first pass waiting on an outside review; the year will move as the ranges are argued, and every move is dated with its reason.`} <a href="/method/">How the numbers are worked out.</a>
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>{h.reason} <a href="/method/">How the numbers are worked out.</a></p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="branches">
    <div class="shead"><h2>The eight branches</h2><span>what has to be dealt with, and what is recorded under each</span></div>
    <ul class="branches">
      {branches.map((b) => (
        <li>
          <a class="bcard" href={b.path}>
            <div class="bcard-top"><span class="n">{b.order} of {branches.length}</span><span>{b.leaves === 0 ? 'nothing yet' : plural(b.leaves, 'treatment or tool', 'treatments and tools')}</span></div>
            <div class="bcard-title">{b.name}</div>
            <div class="bcard-sub">{b.meaning}</div>
            <div class="bcard-foot">
              {b.furthest_label ? `Furthest stage: ${b.furthest_label.toLowerCase()}` : 'No stage reached'}
              {b.median_handled_year !== null && <>. Middle estimate: handled by <span class="year">{yearWords(b.median_handled_year)}</span></>}
            </div>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="dim" style="font-size: 14px; margin-top: 12px;">
      A branch counts as handled in the year its second treatment reaches the clinic, so a branch with nothing in the clinic pushes the year out. <a href="/branches/">The tree</a> has the branches one at a time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow" id="records">
    <div class="shead"><h2>What is recorded</h2><span>six kinds of record, each with its own page</span></div>
    <ul class="records">
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/branches/">The treatments and tools</a></h3>
        <p>One page each, under its branch, with the test, who settles it, the stage reached and the estimate of when it reaches the clinic. {c.siblings} are shared with the sister site The Upgrade Tree, and the two records point at each other.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.leaves, 'written so far', 'written so far')}, {c.leaves_in_clinic} already in the clinic</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/claims/">Who promised what</a></h3>
        <p>A dated log of what named people and companies have said about when, each with the primary source and what it commits to, and a verdict filled in as the dates pass. It sits beside the year and moves no number.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.claims, 'claim', 'claims')} by {records.claims_counts.speakers} people and organisations</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/self-experiments/">Other people's public self-experiments</a></h3>
        <p>Who, what they do, what they measure, what they claim, and what the public record actually shows. A claim about one person is a story, not a record, and it moves nothing on the tree.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.experiments, 'written so far', 'written so far')}</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/story/">The story so far</a></h3>
        <p>Every paper, trial report and regulator's record that bears on the tree, dated, each saying what it settled or moved. The ones that moved nothing are listed beside them and given the same space.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.publications, 'publication logged', 'publications logged')}</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/reading/">This week's reading</a></h3>
        <p>Every treatment and tool read against the week's news, with a verdict for each. The weeks when nothing moved are kept too; they are the point.</p>
        <span class="count">{c.scans_real === 0 ? 'none has run yet; the first follows the launch' : plural(c.scans_real, 'reading so far', 'readings so far')}</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/method/">How the numbers are worked out</a></h3>
        <p>The model in plain words, every setting with its reason and its strongest alternative, one run written out step by step, and the working documents published as written.</p>
        <span class="count">{h.available ? `computed ${longDate(h.computed_on)}` : 'not computed yet'}</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose" id="who">
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0.4em;">Who is asking</h2>
    <p>
      I also keep <a href="https://beltequation.com" rel="noopener">The Belt Equation</a>, <a href="https://thequietgalaxy.com" rel="noopener">The Quiet Galaxy</a> and <a href="https://theupgradetree.com" rel="noopener">The Upgrade Tree</a>, three sister sites built the same way: one question at the top, a small fixed set of reasons under it, and under each reason the specific things that would have to happen, each with a test, each watched and dated. This one is the fourth.
    </p>
    <p class="status-line">
      Records as of {longDate(records.exported_on)}. Everything on this site is built from a public repository, <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">on GitHub</a>, where every number carries its source and every change its date. {h.available ? `The year was computed on ${longDate(h.computed_on)} and the chance before ${fixed.before} is ${percentWords(h.before_2050)}.` : ''}
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
